# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L199


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
[transferToUnoccupiedPlot](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L199-L226) misses to change the old `toilerState[tokenId].plotId` to the new `plotId`, causing a mess in plots and rewards. The damage can be summarized into:
1. Landlords get their plots locked
2. Users can clear already occupied plots, bricking other users' NFTs

## Proof of Concept
[transferToUnoccupiedPlot](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L199-L226) can be used by a user to transfer his `tokenId` to another plot of the same landlord. 

However when setting the new parameters the function misses one - `toilerState[tokenId].plotId`. On a second change (B -> C), when moving A -> B -> C it won't erase `plotOccupied[landlord][B]` making plot B count as filled, even thought the NFT is now on plot C.

---

When we break the above down, for simplex understanding, we can see that when moving the NFT from plot A to B, since `toilerState[tokenId].plotId` is not set to B, it will remain as A.

Then when moving from B to C, since `toilerState[tokenId].plotId == A` the system will clear A (even thought A was already empty) and move on, without clearing plot B (as it should):

```solidity
   ToilerState memory _toiler = toilerState[tokenId];

   // plot A
   uint256 oldPlotId = _toiler.plotId; 

   ...

   // plotOccupied[landlord][A] = Plot({occupied: false, tokenId: 0});
   plotOccupied[_toiler.landlord][oldPlotId] = Plot({occupied: false, tokenId: 0});

   // plotOccupied[landlord][C] = Plot({occupied: true, tokenId: tokenId});
   plotOccupied[_toiler.landlord][plotId] = Plot({occupied: true, tokenId: tokenId});
```

This can be used by malicious users to fill the landlords plots, lowering his reward generation. It can also accidentally happen, which makes the issue even more dangerous.

Note: Another impact of this issue is that if user2 moves to the previously occupied plot, and user1 unstakes, then the unstake will clear user2 plot.


## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Make sure the `plotId` is updated to the new one:

```diff
     toilerState[tokenId].latestTaxRate = plotMetadata[_toiler.landlord].currentTaxRate;
+    toilerState[tokenId].plotId = plotId;

     emit FarmPlotLeave(_toiler.landlord, tokenId, oldPlotId);
     emit FarmPlotTaken(toilerState[tokenId], tokenId);
```


## Assessed type

Error

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L228


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Landlords can lock their assets for 1 block (only when harvesting), in order to avoid any risk exposures.

## Proof of Concept
In order for stakers to ear rewards, they need to stake to an empty active plot and that plot to be not `dirty` when claiming rewards. The plot is labeled `dirty`  when the current `plotId` is bigger than the available plots the landlord has:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L258-L261
```solidity
   if (_getNumPlots(landlord) < _toiler.plotId) {
      timestamp = plotMetadata[landlord].lastUpdated;
      toilerState[tokenId].dirty = true;
   }
```

Plots are calculated with [_getNumPlots](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L344-L346) doing `weight / price`, where the weight (as seen in [getLockedWeightedValue](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LockManager.sol#L481-L491)) is calculated as `quantity * price`, adjusted to 18 decimals. In short free plots are:
```markdown
number of free plots = token quantity * price per token / price per plot
```

The above means that in order for users to stake to a landlord he needs to lock his own tokens inside the market for a given duration, as seen inside [LockManager::_lock](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LockManager.sol#L349-L373)

```solidity
    if (_lockDuration == 0) {
       _lockDuration = lockdrop.minLockDuration;
    }
    if (lockdrop.start <= uint32(block.timestamp) && lockdrop.end >= uint32(block.timestamp)) {
        if ( _lockDuration < lockdrop.minLockDuration 
           || _lockDuration > uint32(configStorage.getUint(StorageKey.MaxLockDuration))
           ) revert InvalidLockDurationError();
```

However the issue that appears here is that lock duration is only checked if we are inside a `lockdrop` event. If outside one, only only restriction landlords have is:

```solidity
        if (_lockDuration == 0) {
            _lockDuration = lockdrop.minLockDuration;
        }
``` 

However that can also be avoided, by setting `lockDuration` to 1 using [setLockDuration](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LockManager.sol#L247), where there are no checks preventing users from setting it bellow the `lockdrop.minLockDuration`. 

Setting `lockDuration` to 1 second will allow landlords to lock their tokens, stake from anther account to their plots and unlock their tokens in the next block. When they decide to claim their rewards, they can again lock and call [farmPlots](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L228-L230) to claim their rewards.

This will allow landlords to generate rewards while avoiding risk and not contributing to the system.

Example:
1. `lockdrop.minLockDuration` is 7 days, but we are not in a `lockdrop` event
2. Landlord sets his `lockDuration` to 1 second using [setLockDuration](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LockManager.sol#L247).
3. He locks to generate a few free plots and stakes his NFTs to them.
4. On the next block he unlocks and uses his assets for something else.
5. After some time he locks again, farms his plots and unlocks on the next block.

This can be repeated by landlords allowing them to farm tokens from `LandManager` and use their assets for other purposes (staking/borrowing/LP to pools...).


Note that having `lockdrop.minLockDuration` set to 0 will also enable them to use flashloans to borrow -> lock -> stake -> unlock -> repay FL and essentially ear rewards for free.

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The current `duration` validation in [setLockDuration](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/main/src/managers/LockManager.sol#L247) will not suffice, as setting it to 1 second will enable him to change it on the next block. Impose another validation checking every lock for a min time:

```diff
    function setLockDuration(uint256 _duration) external notPaused {
        if (_duration > configStorage.getUint(StorageKey.MaxLockDuration)) {
            revert MaximumLockDurationError();
        }

+       if (_duration < configStorage.getUint(StorageKey.MinLockDuration)) {
+           revert MinimumLockDurationError();
+       }
```





## Assessed type

Invalid Validation

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/94cf468aaabf526b7a8319f7eba34014ccebe7b9/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L99


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In `LandManager::updateTaxRate(uint256)` the lastUpdated variable is not updated upon tax rate updation. As a result the lastUpdated variable is incorrect and in `LandManager::_farmPots` it is used to set a `timestamp` variable used for further calculations:

```solidity                
timestamp = plotMetadata[landlord].lastUpdated;
```

Which later on is used in the same function for a calculation:

```solidity
schnibblesTotal =
                (timestamp - _toiler.lastToilDate) *
                BASE_SCHNIBBLE_RATE;
```

And to set other object fields:
1. `toilerState[tokenId].lastToilDate = timestamp;`
2. `landlordMetadata.lastPetMunchable = uint32(timestamp);`

Causing the saved data to be incorrect and the `schnibblesTotal` to be inaccurate.

## Proof of Concept
1. Deploy and initialize `LandManager` contract
2. Configure initial tax rates by calling relevant functions as usual
3. Call `updateTaxRate(uint256)`

## Tools Used
Solidity

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add the following line of code to the `LandManager::updateTaxRate(uint256)`:

```solidity
    function updateTaxRate(uint256 newTaxRate) external override notPaused {
        (address landlord, ) = _getMainAccountRequireRegistered(msg.sender);
        if (newTaxRate < MIN_TAX_RATE || newTaxRate > MAX_TAX_RATE)
            revert InvalidTaxRateError();
        if (plotMetadata[landlord].lastUpdated == 0)
            revert PlotMetadataNotUpdatedError();
        uint256 oldTaxRate = plotMetadata[landlord].currentTaxRate;
        plotMetadata[landlord].currentTaxRate = newTaxRate;
+       plotMetadata[landlord].lastUpdated = block.timestamp;
        emit TaxRateChanged(landlord, oldTaxRate, newTaxRate);
    }
```


## Assessed type

Other

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/94cf468aaabf526b7a8319f7eba34014ccebe7b9/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L312


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In `LandManager::_removeTokenIdFromStakedList` is internal and is missing access control, everyone can call the function via a contract which inherits the `LandManager` and passing on the mainAccount address and removing any tokenId they desire (or all of them).

High - remove other peoples' tokenIds

## Proof of Concept
1. Deploy and initialize `LandManager` contract
2. Configure initial tax rates by calling relevant functions as usual
3. The Attacker creates a contract which inherits the `LandManager`
4. The Attacker uses the before mentioned contract (step 3) to call from it `LandManager::_removeTokenIdFromStakedList` passing on a valid mainAccount and tokenId

## Tools Used
Solidity

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add something like:

```solidity
    function _removeTokenIdFromStakedList(
        address mainAccount,
        uint256 tokenId
    ) internal {
+       require(msg.sender == mainAccount, "Invalid user, cannot remove tokenID from staked list");

        uint256 stakedLength = munchablesStaked[mainAccount].length;
        bool found = false;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < stakedLength; i++) {
            if (munchablesStaked[mainAccount][i] == tokenId) {
                munchablesStaked[mainAccount][i] = munchablesStaked[
                    mainAccount
                ][stakedLength - 1];
                found = true;
                munchablesStaked[mainAccount].pop();
                break;
            }
        }

        if (!found) revert InvalidTokenIdError();
    }
```

Thus, ensuring the function cannot be called by everyone.

Please note that in the example above you may decide to add some more logic to allow an admin to be able to execute `_removeTokenIdFromStakedList` as a backup as well.


## Assessed type

Access Control

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/94cf468aaabf526b7a8319f7eba34014ccebe7b9/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L142


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The protocol includes a feature that enables accounts to create sub-accounts, allowing users to access the application on their mobile devices, for example. These sub-accounts function as fully-fledged accounts, albeit with certain restrictions. From the current codebase, it is evident that sub-accounts can become owners of munchables. However, if a sub-account becomes the owner of the NFT, neither the main account nor the sub-account will be able to utilize the `LandManager::stakeMunchable(...)` functionality, as it will always compare the main account with the owner of the NFT, which is the sub-account.

## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-munchables/blob/94cf468aaabf526b7a8319f7eba34014ccebe7b9/src/managers/LandManager.sol#L142

The following example can be added to `stakeMunchable.test.ts`:

```typescript
it("sub account NFT cannot be staked by main account", async () => {
      await registerPlayer({
        account: sasuke,
        testContracts,
      });
      await registerSubAccount({
        account: sasuke,
        subAccount: naruto,
        testContracts,
      });
      assert.equal(
        (await testContracts.accountManagerProxy.contract.read.getPlayer([naruto]))[0],
        sasuke
      );

      await mockNFTOverlord.write.addReveal([naruto, 100], { account: naruto });
      await mockNFTOverlord.write.startReveal([naruto], { account: naruto });
      await mockNFTOverlord.write.reveal([naruto, 4, 12], { account: naruto }); // NFT number 15

      assert.equal(await testContracts.munchNFT.contract.read.ownerOf([15]), naruto);

      await testContracts.munchNFT.contract.write.approve(
        [testContracts.landManagerProxy.contract.address, 15],
        { account: naruto }
      );

      await assert.rejects(
        testContracts.landManagerProxy.contract.simulate.stakeMunchable([alice, 15, 0], {
          account: sasuke,
        }),
        (err: Error) => assertContractFunctionRevertedError(err, "InvalidOwnerError")
      );
     await assert.rejects(
        testContracts.landManagerProxy.contract.simulate.stakeMunchable([alice, 15, 0], {
          account: naruto,
        }),
        (err: Error) => assertContractFunctionRevertedError(err, "InvalidOwnerError")
      );
    });
```

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add a new function in the `AccountManager.sol` to check if an account is actually a sub-account and then check if the NFT owner is either owned by the main account or if the owner is a sub-account of the main account.

Add the following to `AccountManager.sol` (also add it to the interface):

```solidity
function isSubAccount(address _player, address _subAccount) external view returns (bool isSubAccount) {
        uint256 subAccountsLength = subAccounts[_player].length;

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < subAccountsLength; i++) {
            if (subAccounts[_player][i] == _subAccount) {
                isSubAccount = true;
                break;
            }
        }
    }
```

